# Rationale

Demand Intelligence represents the architectural transition between Enterprise Data Engineering and Enterprise AI Engineering.

Separating business intelligence generation from forecasting implementation establishes a clear architectural boundary where:

- Enterprise Data Engineering produces trusted enterprise information.
- Enterprise Demand Intelligence produces standardized predictive intelligence.
- Enterprise AI Engineering consumes consistent forecasting inputs.

This architectural decision directly supports:

- Enterprise Forecast Dataset Framework
- Enterprise Forecast Modeling Framework
- Enterprise Forecast Algorithm Library
- Enterprise Training Framework
- Enterprise Evaluation Framework
- Enterprise Inference Framework
- Enterprise Model Registry

It also enables future Feature Store integration, explainable AI, reusable forecasting workflows, and enterprise decision intelligence.

---

# Benefits

## Standardized Predictive Features

Every forecasting algorithm consumes identical business intelligence.

---

## Model Independence

Business intelligence remains independent of forecasting implementation.

New forecasting algorithms require no changes to enterprise feature engineering.

---

## Reproducibility

Demand Intelligence datasets can always be regenerated from certified Gold datasets.

---

## Reusability

The same predictive intelligence supports:

- forecasting
- capacity planning
- workforce simulations
- executive reporting
- AI assistants

---

## Explainability

Business features remain understandable to operational stakeholders because they are generated through standardized enterprise logic.

---

## Scalability

Additional intelligence domains can be introduced without modifying forecasting models.

---

# Trade-offs

The architecture introduces:

- additional processing
- additional storage
- metadata management
- governance overhead

These trade-offs are acceptable because they significantly improve consistency, explainability, and enterprise AI readiness.

---

# Alternatives Considered

## Feature Engineering Inside Each Forecasting Model

**Decision:** Rejected

Reasons:

- duplicated logic
- inconsistent features
- difficult maintenance
- unreliable model comparison

---

## Direct Forecasting from Gold Layer

**Decision:** Rejected

Reasons:

- Gold datasets are analytical rather than predictive
- fragmented feature engineering
- reduced explainability
- poor scalability

---

## Notebook-Specific Feature Engineering

**Decision:** Rejected

Reasons:

- inconsistent implementation
- duplicated business logic
- difficult governance
- poor reuse

---

# Consequences

## Positive Consequences

The decision establishes:

- reusable predictive intelligence
- standardized feature engineering
- improved experimentation
- greater explainability
- cleaner architecture
- easier maintenance
- enterprise AI readiness

Demand Intelligence becomes a permanent enterprise capability rather than notebook-specific preprocessing.

